UPDATE public.guides SET body = $thickbodyB$# EU energy labels for smartphones and tablets: what sellers must show

Since 20 June 2025, smartphones and slate tablets on the EU market must carry the EU energy label under Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/1669. The label shows battery endurance, a repairability class from A to E, and protection ratings, with full data behind a QR code in EPREL. Suppliers register the data; dealers, including online sellers, display the label before purchase.

## Key facts

- Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/1669 requires the EU energy label for smartphones and slate tablets placed on the market from 20 June 2025.
- The label shows the energy efficiency class on the familiar A to G scale, battery endurance per full charge, a repairability class from A (most repairable) to E (least repairable), battery durability in charge cycles, repeated free-fall reliability, and ingress protection.
- A QR code on the label links to the product's data in EPREL, the European Product Registry for Energy Labelling, where suppliers must register the product information.
- Suppliers must provide the label and the product information sheet; dealers must display them at the point of sale, including electronically in online shops before the purchase is concluded.
- The companion ecodesign Regulation (EU) 2023/1670 sets binding minimum requirements from the same date: drop resistance, battery durability of 800 cycles at 80% capacity, spare parts for 7 years, and operating system updates for 5 years.
- Exemptions include tablets with detachable keyboards, devices with flexible main displays, and smartphones for high-security communication.

## 1. What the label shows

The energy label for smartphones and tablets adapts the familiar EU label format to the realities of mobile devices. It displays the energy efficiency class from A to G, the battery endurance in hours and minutes per full charge, the repairability class from A to E, the battery's endurance in charge-discharge cycles, the repeated free-fall reliability class, and the ingress protection rating against dust and water. The repairability score is a first for EU energy labelling: it makes how easy a device is to fix visible at the point of sale.

The QR code printed on the label links to the product's EPREL entry, where consumers and authorities can find the full product information sheet. The label must be supplied with the product and displayed wherever the product is offered for sale; for online sales, the electronic label and product information sheet must be shown in proximity to the price, before the consumer commits to the purchase.

## 2. Who must comply: suppliers and dealers

The supplier, normally the manufacturer or the importer placing the product on the EU market, must register the product in EPREL, generate the label from the registered data, and provide the label and product information sheet to dealers. The data behind the label must be supported by testing and technical documentation held by the supplier.

The dealer, any retailer or online seller offering the product to end users, must display the label and make the product information sheet available. For distance selling, including online shops and marketplaces, the electronic versions must be displayed clearly before purchase. A marketplace seller that is itself the dealer carries these duties; platforms should ensure their listing templates can carry the label data.

## 3. The ecodesign companion: binding minimum requirements

The energy label is the information layer; Regulation (EU) 2023/1670 is the performance layer, applying from the same date, 20 June 2025. It sets minimum ecodesign requirements for smartphones, other mobile phones, cordless phones and slate tablets:

- Resistance to accidental drops and scratches and protection against dust and water.
- Battery durability: batteries must withstand at least 800 charge and discharge cycles while retaining at least 80% of their initial capacity.
- Disassembly and repair: critical spare parts must be available to repairers within 5 to 10 working days, until 7 years after the end of sales of the product model on the EU market.
- Operating system upgrades for at least 5 years after the product model is placed on the market.
- Non-discriminatory access for professional repairers to the software and firmware needed for repairs.

Devices that do not meet these minimum requirements cannot be placed on the EU market at all, regardless of the label. The regulation also forbids defeat devices: products designed to alter their behaviour under test conditions.

## 4. Testing, EPREL registration and change control

Compliance starts with testing to the defined methods: battery endurance and cycle durability, drop-test reliability, ingress protection, and the repairability scoring methodology. Test reports must support every value on the label and in EPREL. The supplier registers the product model in EPREL with the full product information sheet data before placing it on the market.

Change control is essential. A hardware revision, a battery supplier change, or a software update that affects energy consumption or battery behaviour can change the labelled values. Suppliers need a process that re-tests or re-evaluates the label data on every relevant product change and updates EPREL before the changed product is placed on the market. Dealers should require confirmation from suppliers that the label data they display is current.

## 5. Compliance checklist

- Confirm the product is in scope: smartphones and slate tablets, noting the exemptions for detachable-keyboard tablets, rollable displays and high-security devices.
- Test to the defined methods for energy, battery endurance, cycle durability, drop reliability, ingress protection and repairability.
- Register the model in EPREL with the complete product information sheet before first placement.
- Generate the label from EPREL data and supply it with the product and to dealers.
- Verify ecodesign minimums under Regulation (EU) 2023/1670: 800 cycles at 80%, 7-year spare parts, 5-year OS updates, repairer access.
- Display the electronic label and product information sheet in online shops before purchase.
- Run change control: re-evaluate label data on hardware, battery or relevant software changes and update EPREL.

| Label element | What it shows | Source of the value |
|---|---|---|
| Energy efficiency class | A (best) to G | Tested energy consumption |
| Battery endurance | Hours and minutes per full charge | Standardised battery test |
| Repairability class | A (most repairable) to E (least repairable) | Repairability scoring method |
| Battery durability | Charge-discharge cycles | Cycle test, minimum 800 at 80% under ecodesign rules |
| Repeated free-fall reliability | Drop resistance class | Standardised drop testing |
| Ingress protection | Dust and water protection rating | IP testing |
| QR code | Link to EPREL product data | Supplier registration |

## 6. What this means for businesses

The smartphone and tablet rules are the template for where EU product regulation is heading: minimum durability and repairability performance plus scored, public information at the point of sale. For brands, the label is a product-design input, not just a packaging task: the repairability class and battery durability figures are determined years earlier in engineering decisions. For importers and online sellers, the duties are immediate and operational: registered data, correct labels, electronic display before purchase, and change control with suppliers.

## How the EU compares with other markets

The **EU** combines a mandatory energy label with scored repairability and binding ecodesign minimums for phones and tablets. The **United States** has no equivalent federal labelling or durability mandate for smartphones; repairability is addressed through state right-to-repair laws. The **United Kingdom** has not adopted the EU's phone and tablet label or ecodesign rules. **Australia's** energy labelling covers other product categories, not smartphones. The EU regime is currently the most demanding in the world for this product category.

### When did the label become mandatory?

From 20 June 2025 for smartphones and slate tablets placed on the EU market, under Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/1669, alongside the ecodesign requirements of Regulation (EU) 2023/1670.

### Who registers in EPREL?

The supplier, normally the manufacturer or the EU importer placing the product on the market. Dealers display the label and product information sheet but do not register.

### Must online shops show the label?

Yes. The electronic label and product information sheet must be displayed in proximity to the price, before the consumer concludes the purchase.

### What if the product changes after launch?

Re-evaluate the label data. Hardware, battery or relevant software changes can alter the declared values; update testing and EPREL before placing the changed product on the market.

### Do refurbished phones need the label?

The duties attach to placing on the market. Refurbishers placing products on the market should assess their obligations under the labelling and ecodesign rules and seek advice for their specific model.

### Where can consumers verify the data?

Through the QR code on the label, which links to the product's public entry in the EPREL database.

## Official sources

- [Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/1669 on EUR-Lex (energy labelling)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023R1669)
- [Regulation (EU) 2023/1670 on EUR-Lex (ecodesign)](https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023R1670)
- [European Commission: new rules for smartphones and tablets](https://single-market-economy.ec.europa.eu/news/new-eu-rules-durable-energy-efficient-and-repairable-smartphones-and-tablets-start-applying-2025-06-20_en)

Last verified: 2026-09-29. Confirm the current EPREL procedures and test methods on the Commission's pages before registering a product.
